  "figure_descriptions": [
    {
      "element": "446:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 2,
      "alt": "Figure 1: Screenshot from 2018 of some curated content\nfrom users’ posts in Snapchat, e.g., “So Satisfying: The oddly\nstatisfying cut of this balloon...” (left), and Twitter, e.g., “The\nbest pictures from Coachella 2018” (right).",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "453:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 2,
      "alt": "Figure 2. Common curation pipeline: Research → Select and collect → Interpret individual content → Organize content and annotation → Interpret across content → Narrate. Sifter focuses on the Select and collect stage, shaded darker than the other stages.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "660:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 3,
      "alt": "Figure 3. An expert curator supplies a query to a video set; the input contains tens of thousands of videos. Machine automated filters reduce the set, then a non-expert crowd selects videos and a separate crowd agreement stage refines the selection. A decision asks Enough videos? No loops back to the selecting stage; yes produces dozens of high-quality, thematically relevant videos for evaluation by the expert curator.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "733:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 5,
      "alt": "Figure 4. Sifter’s task page shows a grid of eight video previews in a browser. Callout 1 marks the task instructions and theme keywords at the top. Callout 2 marks progress indicators showing how many videos have been viewed and selected. Callout 3 marks the video grid; callout 4 marks the pointer over a video, where workers can enable audio and click to select. Callout 5 marks the page timer. A green Next button spans the bottom. Videos autoplay silently until a worker hovers over them to hear audio.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "860:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 6,
      "alt": "Face with rolling eyes emoji.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "861:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 6,
      "alt": "Five fire emojis.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "876:0",
      "role": "Formula",
      "page": 6,
      "alt": "Equation 1. Sifter subscript T equals max(t subscript worker 1, selection; t subscript worker 2, selection) plus max(t subscript worker 1, agreement; t subscript worker 2, agreement). That is, the larger of the two workers’ selection times plus the larger of their two agreement times.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "910:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 7,
      "alt": "Figure 5. Paired box plots of relative relevance ratings for compilations C1–C12, Sifter in blue and staff curators in cyan. Ratings are relative to a baseline random sample. Most medians are near 0–2, with substantial overlapping spreads. For C3, the staff-curator median is about 1 while Sifter is near 0; this is the only compilation reported as significantly different after the source’s correction (p<.0042). C12 has among the highest median ratings for both approaches.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "920:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 7,
      "alt": "Figure 6. Strategy occurrences reported by workers: relevance 19; quality 9; quality first then relevance 7; both quality and relevance 7; relevance first then quality 1. Relevance is the most common strategy.",
      "actual_text": ""
    }
  ],
